\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a smooth projective geometrically connected curve $C/\mathbb F_q$, where $q=p^n$ and $p$ is prime, define
\[
Z_C(T)=\exp\left(\sum_{r\ge1}\#C(\mathbb F_{q^r})\frac{T^r}{r}\right)=\frac{P_C(T)}{(1-T)(1-qT)}.
\]
Here $P_C\in\mathbb Z[T]$ has degree $2g$, with $g$ the genus. Computing the zeta function means determining this integer polynomial.
A hyperelliptic curve has a degree-two map to the projective line. In odd characteristic, the imaginary model in the slides is the smooth completion of $y^2=P(x)$ with $P$ monic squarefree of degree $2g+1$, having a single point above infinity. The corresponding real case has the even-degree model and two geometric points above infinity. The symbol $\widetilde O$ suppresses logarithmic factors; the slides consider small characteristic, with $p$ fixed in the complexity estimates.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Extend hyperelliptic zeta computation to the real case and to characteristic $p=2$, retaining the bounds
\[
\text{time }\widetilde O(g^3n^4),\qquad \text{space }\widetilde O(g^3n^3),\qquad q=p^n.
\]
Thus remove both the imaginary-model restriction and the requirement $p\ne2$ from the stated bounds. \href{https://helper.ipam.ucla.edu/publications/SCWS1/SCWS1_6220.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Remove the two model/characteristic restrictions without worsening the displayed hyperelliptic zeta time and memory bounds.

